<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Lab 3: Euler's Method</title>

  <introduction>
    <p component="prod-02"><term>Due: Tuesday, November 10</term></p>
    <p component="prod-04"><term>Due: Monday, November 9</term></p>

    <p>
      <alert>Names:</alert>
    </p>
    
    <p>
      <term>Instructions:</term> The exercises here should be
      completed in groups of 2 or 3 students with one write-up
      submitted from each group.  Be sure to include everyone's name
      on the write-up.
    </p>

    <p>
      We have used Euler's method to help us
      approximate solutions to differential
      equations.  Today we will use spreadsheets
      to implement Euler's method
      (<q>Oiler's method</q>).
    </p>
    <p>
      Remember: the derivative <m>dy/dt</m> gives
      the instantaneous rate of change.  When we
      move forward in time, the change in <m>y</m>
      is approximately
      <md>
        \Delta y \approx \frac{dy}{dt} \times
        \Delta t.
      </md>
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        We'll begin by considering the differential
        equation
        <md>
          \frac{dy}{dt} = 2y
        </md>
        with the initial value <m>y(0) = 3</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        We would like to approximate the solution
        over the interval <m>0\leq t\leq 1</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the solution to this differential equation with
          the appropriate initial value.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Since we now have an explicit formula for
          the solution, we don't really need to build
          a numerical approximation to the solution.
          However, this is a good problem to start
          with since we can test the accuracy of our
          approximation.
        </p>
        <p>
          We will walk from <m>t=0</m> to <m>t=1</m>
          taking steps of size <m>h=0.1</m>; that is,
          we will approximate the solution at
          <m>t=0.1, 0.2, 0.3, \ldots, 1</m>.  We can
          do the first steps as follows.
        </p>
        <p>
          Since <m>y(0) = 3</m> and
          <m>dy/dt = 2y</m>, what is <m>dy/dt</m> when <m>t=0</m>?
          By approximately how much does
          <m>y</m> change when we move from
          <m>t=0</m> to <m>t=0.1</m>?  Approximately
          what is <m>y(0.1)</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now we have an approximation for
          <m>y(0.1)</m>.  What is <m>dy/dt</m> at
          this time?  By approximately how much does
          <m>y</m> change when we move from
          <m>t=0.1</m> to <m>t=0.2</m>?
          Approximately what is <m>y(0.2)</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>
  
  <exercise workspace="0.5in">
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Now we will build a Google sheet that
        continues this approximation process as
        shown below.  Remember that the differential
        equation gives the slope of the tangent
        lines.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A convenient way to set up the spreadsheet is shown below.
        </p>
        <tabular>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center"/>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><m>h</m></cell>
            <cell><m>t</m></cell>
            <cell><m>y</m></cell>
            <cell><m>dy/dt</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\Delta y</m></cell>
            <cell>Exact solution</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>0.1000</cell>
            <cell>0.0000</cell>
            <cell>3.0000</cell>
            <cell>6.0000</cell>
            <cell>0.6000</cell>
            <cell>3.0000</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>0.1000</cell>
            <cell>0.1000</cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>0.1000</cell>
            <cell>0.2000</cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>0.1000</cell>
            <cell>0.3000</cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
        <p>
          Now that you have the change in <m>y</m>,
          how do you find the next value,
          <m>y(0.1)</m>?
        </p>
        <tabular>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center"/>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><m>h</m></cell>
            <cell><m>t</m></cell>
            <cell><m>y</m></cell>
            <cell>Slope</cell>
            <cell><m>\Delta y</m></cell>
            <cell>Exact solution</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>0.1000</cell>
            <cell>0.0000</cell>
            <cell>3.0000</cell>
            <cell>6.0000</cell>
            <cell>0.6000</cell>
            <cell>3.0000</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>0.1000</cell>
            <cell>0.1000</cell>
            <cell>3.6000</cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>0.1000</cell>
            <cell>0.2000</cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>0.1000</cell>
            <cell>0.3000</cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
        <p>
          Now continue this process:
        </p>
        <tabular>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center"/>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><m>h</m></cell>
            <cell><m>t</m></cell>
            <cell><m>y</m></cell>
            <cell>Slope</cell>
            <cell><m>\Delta y</m></cell>
            <cell>Exact solution</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>0.1000</cell>
            <cell>0.0000</cell>
            <cell>3.0000</cell>
            <cell>6.0000</cell>
            <cell>0.6000</cell>
            <cell>3.0000</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>0.1000</cell>
            <cell>0.1000</cell>
            <cell>3.6000</cell>
            <cell>7.2000</cell>
            <cell>0.7200</cell>
            <cell>3.6642</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>0.1000</cell>
            <cell>0.2000</cell>
            <cell>4.3200</cell>
            <cell>8.6400</cell>
            <cell>0.8640</cell>
            <cell>4.4755</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>0.1000</cell>
            <cell>0.3000</cell>
            <cell>5.1840</cell>
            <cell>10.3680</cell>
            <cell>1.0368</cell>
            <cell>5.4664</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
        <p>
          What does Euler's method give for the
          approximate value of <m>y(1)</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now repeat using a step size of
          <m>h = 0.01</m>.  If your worksheet is set
          up efficiently, you should only have to
          change one entry.  What does Euler's method
          give for the approximate value of
          <m>y(1)</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Finally, repeat again using a step size of
          <m>h = 0.001</m>.  What is your
          approximation for the value of
          <m>y(1)</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Fill in the table below (<q>Error</q> is
          just the difference between the exact value
          and your approximation):
        </p>
        <tabular>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center"/>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Step size</cell>
            <cell>Approximate value of <m>y(1)</m></cell>
            <cell>Error</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>0.1<m>\vtab</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\htab</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\htab</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>0.01<m>\vtab</m></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>0.001<m>\vtab</m></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
        <p>
          Describe quantitatively how the error
          changes as the step size changes.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Now we'll consider the differential equation
        <md>
          \frac{dy}{dt} = \frac{1}{10}(6y - y^2)
        </md>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Plot below a graph of
          <m>\frac{dy}{dt}</m> versus <m>y</m> and
          sketch a slope field.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          If we look at the solution with the initial
          value <m>y(0) = 1</m>, what do you expect
          to happen over a very long time?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          If we look at the solution with the initial
          value <m>y(0) = 8</m>, what do you expect
          to happen over a very long time?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now build a Google sheet implementing
          Euler's method to find the solution to this
          differential equation with <m>y(0) = 1</m>
          using a step size of <m>h = 0.1</m> over
          the range <m>0 \leq t \leq 12</m>.  Plot
          the approximate solution as a function of
          time and include a sketch below.  Does this
          verify your expectation from above?
          Explain.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          By looking at the graph of your approximate
          solution, what is the value of <m>y</m>
          when the slope is the greatest?  Offer an
          explanation of this by looking at your
          graph of <m>\frac{dy}{dt}</m> versus
          <m>y</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1.0in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now look at the solution with
          <m>y(0) = 8</m>.  If you have your
          worksheet set up efficiently, you should
          only have to change one entry.  Include a
          sketch of your solution below.  Does the
          long-term behavior verify your expectation?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
